{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":74380,"duration_api_ms":92754,"num_turns":4,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0f81582a-8b83-4186-a98a-0d45eaf20799","total_cost_usd":0.3213661,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24988,"cache_read_input_tokens":77815,"output_tokens":1991,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":24988,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":58,"outputTokens":5128,"cacheReadInputTokens":188481,"cacheCreationInputTokens":25554,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0764886,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":1991,"cacheReadInputTokens":77815,"cacheCreationInputTokens":24988,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.24487750000000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Accept three amendments that fix real behavioral or documentary inconsistencies. Reject the VS-07 denitrification param zeroing because keeping identical process params across arms is the correct controlled-experiment design—only substrate presence should differ.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-a2e51b55","decision":"reject","rationale":"The test's design intentionally keeps denitrification process params identical across both arms so that substrate presence is the sole independent variable. Zeroing the params on the bare arm would introduce a second difference, weakening the control. The existing comment ('Remove substrate entirely to eliminate any denitrification pathway') already documents the intent. The concern about a hypothetical future water-column denitrification pathway is speculative; if such a pathway were added, it would require its own parameterization and test coverage, not a defensive zero here."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-98b4d260","decision":"accept","rationale":"The report template at lines 508-511 includes `maturity=X.XX` in VS-01 checkpoint lines, but the scenario documentation at lines 69-72 explicitly states cycling is validated by nitrification output, not the maturity index. This creates a genuine inconsistency: anyone reading the template or automation filling it will treat maturity as a pass/fail criterion when it is not. The template should either drop the maturity field or annotate it as observational-only."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-e53a714b","decision":"accept","rationale":"At line 559, `cadence_temp_factor` applies `.max(0.01)` after `.max(params.temp_condition_min_factor)`, so even when validation accepts `temp_condition_min_factor = 0.0`, the runtime silently clamps to 0.01. This means readiness continues to accumulate (`timer_days / effective_interval` remains positive) and molts eventually resolve, contradicting the configuration intent of a true zero floor. The hidden floor must be removed or replaced with explicit zero-handling (e.g., skip readiness accumulation when the factor is zero)."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-c1750e41","decision":"accept","rationale":"In `emit_molt_failure` (line ~1380), `gh_ratio = gh_d / params.gh_min_d.max(0.01)` and `critical_gh_failure = gh_ratio < params.critical_molt_gh_ratio` lack the `params.gh_min_d > 0.0` guard that molt_cycle uses at line 555. When `gh_min_d` is disabled (0.0), this computes `0.0 / 0.01 = 0.0`, which is always below `critical_molt_gh_ratio`, so every failed molt emits a bogus 'critical GH ratio' detail even though GH-based hard failure is disabled. The guard must match molt_cycle's logic to keep diagnostics truthful."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"27caca50-55f6-428a-9ef6-1cee56d8c9be"}
